## Agents

The setup wizard chose the model that new chats use, and tested that it answers.

In most programs, a chat sidebar and an application share a screen but stay separate systems. The assistant sees summaries and a few narrow actions, while the application keeps the real state somewhere else.

Compos begins with one environment. The application and the conversation are buffers in the same group. You and the agent act on the same named commands and the same state.

The commands in this section are optional. Your provider may charge for requests. A **chat** is the conversation buffer. A **model** writes responses. An **agent** uses that model with tools to work in Compos.

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `M-x chat-new` | Start a new chat buffer |
| `C-c b` | Choose the chat's connector, model, effort, tools and permissions |
| `M-x agent-switch` | Move a running chat to another connector and model |
| `M-o` | Open the chat of the buffer you are in |
| `C-c w` | Switch between a work buffer and its group's chat |

`M-|` sends the active region through an LLM.

A **connector** is how Compos reaches an agent or model. The connector picker shows the supported connectors below. A connector works when its program and credentials are available, or when its browser model can run. Test a reply before you rely on the connector.

| Connector | What it needs |
| --- | --- |
| `codex-app-server` | Codex through `codex app-server`. It uses the Codex CLI's existing ChatGPT login; it needs no API key. |
| `claude-code` | Claude Code through ACP. It needs a configured adapter and a Claude login. |
| `opencode` | OpenCode through ACP. It uses the providers configured in OpenCode. |
| `deepseek` | DeepSeek Harness through ACP. It needs an installed and configured DSH profile. |
| `api` | Direct model APIs for providers whose keys you configured in Compos. |
| `gemini-nano` | The local browser model, when the browser provides it. |

ACP is the Agent Client Protocol. It lets Compos start an external coding agent as a process and exchange structured messages, tools and permissions with it. Codex now uses its app-server protocol instead. The older `codex-acp` connector is deprecated and is not shown in the picker.

> **Try it.** Type `M-x chat-new` to start a chat.

> **Try it.** In the chat, type `C-c b`. A menu opens over the screen with this chat's connector, model and tools. Look at it, then press `ESC` to close it.

> **Try it.** Type a message in the chat and press `RET` to send it.

To give one chat another model, open that menu again: `b` chooses the connector and `m` the model, and `ESC` gives the setup to the chat. If a chat answers 401, the connector needs its login: `M-x setup-ai-guide` explains how.

Most buffers have a chat of their own. `M-o` opens it beside the buffer, and starts one when there is none. The chat reads the buffer it belongs to, so you never paste the text into it.

> **Try it.** In the practice page, type `M-o`. Ask the chat: "What is this page about?"

A group has a chat too, which sees every buffer in the group. `C-c w` goes to it from any buffer, and back again.

> **Try it.** Type `C-c w` to go to the group's chat. Type `C-c w` again to come back.

Agents also write extensions. Ask for one and watch it appear as a command:

> **Try it.** Type `M-o` and ask: "Define a Scheme command named tutorial-agent-hello that prints a greeting." When it answers, type `M-x tutorial-agent-hello`.

The agent wrote the definition with the same Scheme evaluator the editor itself runs. The context is concrete: it is there in the buffer.

