## Prefix arguments

Most Compos commands accept a count. To give one, type `C-u` and then the digits, before you type the command. With a META key there is a shorter way: hold META and type the digits.

> **Try it.** Type `C-u 5 <down>`. Point moves down five lines. Type `M-3 <up>` to move back three lines.

A command uses the count in different ways. Movement, deletion, killing, scrolling and undo repeat. A command that takes no count may read `C-u` as a request for another version of itself.

> **Try it.** Type `C-u 5 C-v` to scroll five lines. Then type `C-u 5 M-v` to scroll back.

You do not need prefix arguments often. The point is that **commands compose**: a small set of movements and edits expresses large actions.

## Modes and keymaps

A **major mode** gives a buffer its main interpretation: text, Scheme, chat, browse, help, a list. A **minor mode** adds separate behaviour, such as previews or editing conventions.

Behaviour is not fixed to a set of application types. It composes from named maps, modes, hooks, buffer-local values and commands.

When you type a key, Compos looks for it in this order: temporary maps, the thing at point, minor modes, the buffer and its major mode, read-only behaviour, then global bindings. Context can change a familiar key without throwing away the common interface.

> **Try it.** Type `C-h B`. The keymap browser shows every binding in force here, which map supplied it, and whether another binding shadows it. Type `f` and a word, such as "tutorial" or "scroll", to filter the list. Type `q` to leave.

So a list, a source file, a chat and a browser page can differ where they should, and stay consistent where they can.

## Lists and previews

Compos applications share one list interface:

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `n` | Move to the next row |
| `p` | Move to the previous row |
| `/` | Narrow the rows as you type |
| Backslash | Remove the newest filter |
| `SPC` | Mark items, when the list supports selection |
| `g` | Refresh, when the list supports refreshing |

A list mode adds its own actions, such as `RET` to visit a row and `q` to quit. A list mode can also give a shared key a different action. The up and down arrows always move by rows.

> **Try it.** Type `M-x ibuffer`. This list manages buffers. Move through the rows with the up and down arrows. In ibuffer, `p` turns the row preview on and off. Type `f` and part of a buffer name, press `RET` to accept the filter, then press Backslash to widen the list again.

Every buffer opens normally, in a window, as your own. A list such as dired opens the file at point in another window with `RET`, while your selection stays in the list. A list of buffers shows the row at point as a **preview**: a card that floats over the frame and follows the highlight. `q` dismisses the card. `M-<down>` and `M-<up>` scroll the other window without taking your focus.

This is `scroll-other-window` made into a pattern: look at nearby context without losing the work that led you there.

> **Try it.** Type `C-x b`, choose `*tutorial*` and press `RET` to bring the practice page back.

## Browsing the web

`M-x browse` opens a URL, or a search, as readable Markdown in a browse buffer. The page on the web stays the source of truth, and preview mode gives it a graphical reading surface.

> **Try it.** Type `M-x browse` and enter `https://example.com`. When the page appears, try `TAB`, `n` and `p` to move among links. Type `q` to return to the tutorial.

In browse mode:

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `RET` | Follow a link in the current browse tab |
| `s-RET` | Open a separate browse tab (Command-RET on macOS) |
| `M-RET` | Open the link in another window |
| `l` | Go back (Meta-Left does the same) |
| Meta-Right | Go forward |
| `R` | Toggle calm and full readings |
| `o` | Open the page in the outside browser |
| `w` | Copy the link at point, or the page URL |
| `H` | Open the reading history |
| `C-c C-v` | Cycle the view: monospace, serif, then Markdown source |

The web is not reduced to a screenshot, and an agent does not have to guess at DOM selectors. The readable page has a buffer, named commands, links, history, point and a mode, like everything else here.

## Why Compos

Most programs make you leave your work to find the next piece of information. You switch to a browser, then to a chat window, then to a terminal, and you rebuild the task in your head each time.

**Compos does it differently. The information comes to you**, in another window, without your leaving what you are doing.

Emacs, the editor Compos is modelled on, was built this way long before agents existed. A programmer could keep code in one window and documentation in another, and read the documentation without leaving the code. Mail, help, shells, directories and source all lived in the same program, and the same movement and editing commands worked in all of them.

Compos keeps that idea and adds a graphical display, work that runs at the same time, saved state, and agents. An **agent** is a model that works on the same buffers you do. A web page, a file, a chat, a mail thread, a search result and a running process are all buffers in one system.

Every one of them has a name, named commands, a place on the screen, and state that you and an agent can both change.

Every window, list and page uses the same movement and editing keys. So the next sections teach those keys, on the text in front of you. When they are familiar, the sections on Windows, Lists and previews, Groups and Agents show the idea above in action.

The tutorial is a buffer too. You are learning Compos from inside Compos.

## Persistence

Compos runs as a daemon. It does not lose its state like a web page that you close.

Durable buffers, frame window trees, points, chats, groups and selected local state survive a restart. Compos puts back the saved theme, and browser clients reconnect to their frames.

Buffer checkpoints keep file paths and live contents, including unsaved edits. Buffers that visit no file are checkpointed too. A mode rebuilds its live behaviour around the restored state.

Your work is not a form submission, and your chat is not an archive behind a settings page. **When you leave and return, the world you built is still there.**

The tutorial follows the same rule. Normal checkpoints preserve a live TUTORIAL buffer without asking. When its point or text has changed, `C-x k` asks whether to keep this copy and position for `C-h t`.

One thing does not come back this way: a definition you add at runtime. The next section explains why.

## The editor is malleable

Compos is not only a fixed editor. It is a live Scheme environment. Loading a file is evaluation, and a symbol is a stable name whose definition can change while the system runs. The evaluator is part of the product.

| Key | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `M-:` | Evaluate one Scheme expression |
| `C-x C-e` | Evaluate the expression before point, in a Scheme buffer |

> **Try it.** Type `M-:`, enter the expression below, and press `RET`.

```scheme
(message "Hello from the live Scheme environment")
```

The message appears at once. No build, restart, bundler or plugin host was needed.

You can make a command the same way.

> **Try it.** Type `M-:`, enter the following expression as one line, and press `RET`.

```scheme
(define-command "tutorial-hello" "Say hello from the tutorial" (lambda () (message "The running editor has a new command")))
```

> **Try it.** Now type `M-x tutorial-hello`.

The definition lasts until you restart, unless you put it in `~/.compos/init.scm` or in a project's `compos.scm`. A buffer holds data, and the daemon brings data back from its checkpoints. A definition is part of the running program, and the program starts again from your init files. So try a change live first, then keep it when it proves useful. An agent can make the same small extension for you.

In Compos, a missing workflow can begin as a few lines of Scheme and grow only as far as its usefulness demands.

## Scheme and the BEAM

One rule explains the whole system:

> **Elixir supplies mechanism. Scheme decides policy.**

Scheme owns what you experience as the editor: commands, keymaps, modes, hooks, completion, display rules, themes, chats, mail, browser policy, permission prompts and applications.

Elixir and OTP own the mechanisms that move bytes, cross operating-system boundaries, or need reliable concurrent lifetimes: ropes, parsers, sockets, PTYs, schedulers, model transport, buffers as processes, and supervision.

So Compos needs no large plugin lifecycle framework of the JavaScript kind. Lisp already supplies symbols, late binding, live evaluation, dynamic context and inspectable state. Ordinary definitions and registrations stay ordinary. External resources such as processes, sockets and timers get explicit owners through OTP.

The boundary keeps the system open without making the runtime fragile. Scheme can redefine what a key means while the editor runs. OTP can stop a slow model stream, or a failing process, from freezing the interface.

The browser is a renderer and a client, not the editor. The headless daemon is the system of record. The browser UI, the desktop shell and RPC clients address the same core, and another client, such as a TUI, can use it too.

